tic zone, soil class and experiment age (i.e. years since beginning NT). Our study included 41 papers, with a total number of 90 case studies, that covered arable lands of all five continents. On average, NT significantly decreased CH4 emissions from paddy fields, from 12.39 to 9.55 mg m\u22122 h-1 (p &lt; 0.05). Conversely, NT showed a slight but non-significant tendency to increase CH4 emissions in maize-cultivated fields, from an average of -0.15 mg m\u22122 h-1 of CT to 0.05 mg m\u22122 h-1 of NT. Other factors moderating the NT effect – like climate, soil, or years since the conversion to NT management – had weak regulation\n######################\nOutput:'}
